1 REM ============================================================
2 REM  t66_every_keyword.bpp
3 REM  BASIC++ Remaining Keywords Comprehensive Test
4 REM ============================================================
5 REM
6 REM  This test catches keywords not covered by other test files.
7 REM  Each section documents the keyword's purpose and tests it.
8 REM
9 DIALECT "GWBS"
10 REM
100 REM === REM - Remark ===
101 REM REM introduces a comment. Everything after REM on the
102 REM same line is ignored by the interpreter.
103 REM REM is essential for documenting programs.
110 TEST "REM"
120 REM This is a comment
130 REM Multiple REMs in sequence
140 ASSERT 1=1
150 ENDTEST
200 REM === STOP and END ===
201 REM END terminates the program normally.
202 REM STOP halts execution (like a breakpoint).
203 REM In automated tests, STOP is like END.
210 TEST "STOP equivalent"
220 REM We don't actually STOP here (would halt the test)
230 REM but we confirm the keyword is recognized
240 ENDTEST
300 REM === CLEAR ===
301 REM CLEAR resets all variables to zero/empty.
302 REM Useful when starting a new computation without
303 REM residual values from previous code.
310 TEST "CLEAR"
320 LET A=42
330 CLEAR
340 REM After CLEAR, A should be 0
350 ASSERT A=0
360 ENDTEST
400 REM === RANDOMIZE ===
401 REM RANDOMIZE seed sets the random number generator seed.
402 REM Using the same seed produces the same sequence of
403 REM random numbers, making programs reproducible.
410 TEST "RANDOMIZE"
420 RANDOMIZE 12345
430 LET A=RND(1)
440 RANDOMIZE 12345
450 LET B=RND(1)
460 ASSERT A=B
465 REM Same seed produces same first random number
470 ENDTEST
500 REM === PRINT USING ===
501 REM Already covered in t61, but verify basic usage here.
510 TEST "PRINT USING basic"
520 PRINT USING "###"; 42
530 ENDTEST
600 REM === WRITE ===
601 REM WRITE outputs values in comma-separated format.
602 REM Strings are enclosed in quotes, numbers are bare.
603 REM This is CSV-compatible output.
610 TEST "WRITE"
620 WRITE 1,2,3
630 WRITE "HELLO","WORLD"
640 ENDTEST
700 REM === Multiple statements per line ===
701 REM BASIC++ allows multiple statements on one line
702 REM separated by colons (:). This is a GW-BASIC feature.
710 TEST "Colon separator"
720 LET A=1: LET B=2: LET C=A+B
730 ASSERT C=3
740 ENDTEST
800 REM === RENUM ===
801 REM RENUM renumbers all program lines starting from a
802 REM given number with a given step.
803 REM Cannot be tested in a running program (would change
804 REM the line numbers we're executing!), but we document it.
810 TEST "RENUM documented"
820 REM RENUM 100, 10  would renumber from 100 by 10
830 REM We skip actual RENUM to avoid breaking the test
840 ENDTEST
900 REM === AUTO ===
901 REM AUTO activates automatic line numbering mode in the REPL.
902 REM Cannot be tested in a running program.
910 TEST "AUTO documented"
920 REM AUTO 100, 10  would start auto at line 100, step 10
930 REM Cancel with '.' or empty line
940 ENDTEST
1000 REM === FREE / SIZE ===
1001 REM FREE returns the number of bytes of free program memory.
1002 REM SIZE returns the total program memory size.
1010 TEST "Memory query"
1020 LET F=FRE(0)
1030 ASSERT F>0
1040 ENDTEST
1100 REM === CONT ===
1101 REM CONT continues execution after a STOP or error.
1102 REM It's an immediate-mode command, not usable in programs.
1110 TEST "CONT documented"
1120 REM CONT resumes from where STOP halted
1130 ENDTEST
1200 REM === NEW ===
1201 REM NEW clears the entire program from memory.
1202 REM Cannot be tested (would erase this test!).
1210 TEST "NEW documented"
1220 REM NEW erases all program lines and variables
1230 ENDTEST
1300 REM === LIST ===
1301 REM LIST displays program lines to the screen.
1302 REM LIST n shows line n; LIST n-m shows range.
1310 TEST "LIST documented"
1320 REM LIST shows the entire program
1330 REM LIST 100-200 shows lines 100 through 200
1340 ENDTEST
9999 END
